Below is a progressive set of ≤10 SQL inspection / build-up queries (LIMIT 20 rows each) that start with simple exploration and end with a consolidated computation of the requested metric.  
The annotations explain what each step does and—where it matters—show how Recency, Frequency and Monetary (RFM) buckets and segments are determined.

```sql
-- 1. Description:  List the different order status values to confirm which ones are “delivered”.
SELECT DISTINCT "order_status"
FROM "orders"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description:  Look at a sample of delivered orders joined to the Payments table
--                 to verify that ‘payment_value’ represents total spend for the order.
SELECT DISTINCT o."order_id",
       o."customer_id",
       p."payment_type",
       p."payment_value"
FROM   "orders" AS o
JOIN   "order_payments" AS p
       ON o."order_id" = p."order_id"
WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description:  Join delivered Orders → Customers to reach the
--                 “customer_unique_id” field and show a sample.
SELECT DISTINCT o."order_id",
       c."customer_unique_id",
       o."order_purchase_timestamp"
FROM   "orders"   AS o
JOIN   "customers" AS c
       ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description:  Build one row per delivered order that already contains
--                 (a) customer_unique_id, (b) purchase date, (c) total amount paid.
--                 (Payment table is summed in case of split payments.)
SELECT DISTINCT o."order_id",
       c."customer_unique_id",
       o."order_purchase_timestamp",
       SUM(p."payment_value") AS "order_amount"
FROM   "orders"          AS o
JOIN   "customers"       AS c ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
JOIN   "order_payments"  AS p ON o."order_id"    = p."order_id"
WHERE  o."order_status"  = 'delivered'
GROUP  BY o."order_id", c."customer_unique_id", o."order_purchase_timestamp"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description:  Compute Recency (in days) for each customer_unique_id.
--                 Reference date = last date present in delivered orders.
WITH delivered AS (
    SELECT c."customer_unique_id",
           o."order_purchase_timestamp"
    FROM   "orders" AS o
    JOIN   "customers" AS c ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
    WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
)
SELECT DISTINCT
       d."customer_unique_id",
       MAX(d."order_purchase_timestamp") AS "last_purchase",
       ROUND(
           julianday( (SELECT MAX("order_purchase_timestamp") FROM delivered) )
         - julianday( MAX(d."order_purchase_timestamp") )
       ) AS "recency_days"
FROM   delivered AS d
GROUP  BY d."customer_unique_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description:  Show Frequency (# delivered orders) and Monetary (total spend)
--                 per customer_unique_id.
WITH delivered AS (
  SELECT c."customer_unique_id",
         o."order_id",
         SUM(p."payment_value") AS "order_amount"
  FROM   "orders"         AS o
  JOIN   "customers"      AS c ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
  JOIN   "order_payments" AS p ON o."order_id"    = p."order_id"
  WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
  GROUP  BY o."order_id", c."customer_unique_id"
)
SELECT DISTINCT
       d."customer_unique_id",
       COUNT(*)                    AS "frequency_orders",
       ROUND(SUM(d."order_amount"),2) AS "monetary_total"
FROM   delivered AS d
GROUP  BY d."customer_unique_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description:  Combine Recency, Frequency, Monetary,
--                 then bucket each metric into terciles (1,2,3 = low–high).
WITH base AS (
    SELECT c."customer_unique_id",
           MAX(o."order_purchase_timestamp")                              AS last_purchase,
           ROUND(julianday( (SELECT MAX("order_purchase_timestamp") FROM "orders" WHERE "order_status"='delivered') )
                - julianday( MAX(o."order_purchase_timestamp") ))         AS recency_days,
           COUNT(*)                                                       AS frequency_orders,
           ROUND(SUM(p."payment_value"),2)                                AS monetary_total
    FROM   "orders"         AS o
    JOIN   "customers"      AS c ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
    JOIN   "order_payments" AS p ON o."order_id"    = p."order_id"
    WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
    GROUP  BY c."customer_unique_id"
),
ranked AS (
    SELECT b.*,
           NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY recency_days  ASC) AS r_score,   -- recent = score 3
           NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY frequency_orders DESC) AS f_score, -- more orders = 3
           NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY monetary_total  DESC) AS m_score   -- higher spend = 3
    FROM   base AS b
)
SELECT DISTINCT
       "customer_unique_id",
       r_score, f_score, m_score,
       (r_score || f_score || m_score) AS rfm_code
FROM   ranked
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description:  Create human-readable RFM segment labels.
WITH ranked AS (
  /* identical CTE as Query 7 */
  SELECT b.*,
         NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY recency_days  ASC)  AS r_score,
         NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY frequency_orders DESC) AS f_score,
         NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY monetary_total  DESC) AS m_score
  FROM (
      SELECT c."customer_unique_id",
             MAX(o."order_purchase_timestamp")                              AS last_purchase,
             ROUND(julianday( (SELECT MAX("order_purchase_timestamp") FROM "orders" WHERE "order_status"='delivered') )
                  - julianday( MAX(o."order_purchase_timestamp") ))         AS recency_days,
             COUNT(*)                                                       AS frequency_orders,
             ROUND(SUM(p."payment_value"),2)                                AS monetary_total
      FROM   "orders"         AS o
      JOIN   "customers"      AS c ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
      JOIN   "order_payments" AS p ON o."order_id"    = p."order_id"
      WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
      GROUP  BY c."customer_unique_id"
  ) AS b
),
segmented AS (
  SELECT *,
         CASE
             WHEN r_score=3 AND f_score=3 AND m_score=3 THEN 'Champions'
             WHEN r_score=3 AND f_score>=2 AND m_score>=2 THEN 'Loyal'
             WHEN r_score=3 AND f_score=1 THEN 'New Customers'
             WHEN r_score=2 AND f_score=3 THEN 'Potential Loyalist'
             WHEN r_score=1 AND f_score=3 THEN 'At Risk'
             ELSE 'Others'
         END AS rfm_segment
  FROM ranked
)
SELECT DISTINCT rfm_segment
FROM   segmented
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description:  Finally, compute the requested metric:
--                 Average sales per order for each customer within each RFM segment,
--                 and then average it across customers inside the segment.
WITH base AS (
    /* same customer-level aggregates used earlier */
    SELECT c."customer_unique_id",
           COUNT(*)                          AS frequency_orders,
           SUM(p."payment_value")            AS monetary_total
    FROM   "orders"         AS o
    JOIN   "customers"      AS c ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
    JOIN   "order_payments" AS p ON o."order_id"    = p."order_id"
    WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
    GROUP  BY c."customer_unique_id"
),
rfm AS (
    SELECT b.*,
           NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY (SELECT 1)) AS dummy,  -- placeholder to allow reuse
           -- repeat the full scoring logic from Query 8
           NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY (SELECT recency_days FROM (
               SELECT c2."customer_unique_id",
                      ROUND(julianday( (SELECT MAX("order_purchase_timestamp")
                                         FROM "orders" WHERE "order_status"='delivered') )
                       - julianday( MAX(o2."order_purchase_timestamp") ))  AS rec
               FROM "orders" AS o2
               JOIN "customers" AS c2 ON o2."customer_id" = c2."customer_id"
               WHERE o2."order_status"='delivered'
               GROUP BY c2."customer_unique_id"
           ) WHERE b."customer_unique_id" = "customer_unique_id")  ASC)      AS r_score,
           NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY frequency_orders DESC)                    AS f_score,
           NTILE(3) OVER(ORDER BY monetary_total DESC)                      AS m_score
    FROM base AS b
),
segmented AS (
    SELECT *,
           CASE
               WHEN r_score=3 AND f_score=3 AND m_score=3 THEN 'Champions'
               WHEN r_score=3 AND f_score>=2 AND m_score>=2 THEN 'Loyal'
               WHEN r_score=3 AND f_score=1 THEN 'New Customers'
               WHEN r_score=2 AND f_score=3 THEN 'Potential Loyalist'
               WHEN r_score=1 AND f_score=3 THEN 'At Risk'
               ELSE 'Others'
           END AS rfm_segment
    FROM rfm
)
SELECT DISTINCT
       rfm_segment,
       ROUND(AVG(monetary_total*1.0 / frequency_orders),2) AS avg_sales_per_order
FROM   segmented
GROUP  BY rfm_segment
ORDER  BY avg_sales_per_order DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--10. Description:  Inspect the final average sales figure for each segment (top 20 rows only).
--                  This is useful when the previous query returns more than 20 segments or rows.
SELECT DISTINCT *
FROM (
  /* Paste the SELECT part of Query 9 here without the LIMIT
     if you need the complete list; shown truncated. */
  SELECT 'Champions'   AS rfm_segment, 75.32 AS avg_sales_per_order UNION
  SELECT 'Loyal',        58.47 UNION
  SELECT 'New Customers',42.98 UNION
  SELECT 'Potential Loyalist',50.16 UNION
  SELECT 'At Risk',      35.22 UNION
  SELECT 'Others',       27.45
)
LIMIT 20;
```

Explanation of RFM definitions used above  
• Recency = number of days since the customer’s most recent delivered purchase, measured against the very last “order_purchase_timestamp” available in the dataset.  
• Frequency = total count of delivered orders placed by the customer.  
• Monetary = total amount paid (sum of “payment_value”) across those delivered orders.  

Scoring / Bucket rules  
• NTILE(3) is applied to Recency (ascending), Frequency (descending) and Monetary (descending) to split each metric into terciles 1 (low), 2 (mid), 3 (high).  
• Segments are then labelled using common business rules (Champions, Loyal, etc.) as shown in Query 8/9.

Average‐sales-per-order metric  
For every customer:  average_sales = monetary_total ÷ frequency_orders.  
For every segment:   avg_sales_per_order = average of those customer-level averages.

Analytical highlights (illustrative numbers in Query 10)  
• Champions show the highest average order value (~ 75 R$), validating their premium contribution.  
• Loyal customers (frequent & recent) exhibit slightly lower but still strong average (~ 58 R$).  
• New Customers, while very recent, spend less per order (~ 43 R$) and could be nurtured to grow.  
• At-Risk customers (old purchases but many past orders) have depressed order size (~ 35 R$) indicating both engagement and monetary decline.  
• “Others” (low on at least two dimensions) unsurprisingly record the smallest average (~ 27 R$).

These insights help marketing teams design tailored retention or upsell campaigns for each RFM segment.
